@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix xml: <http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix core: <http://fairness_ontology.com/> .
@prefix papers: <http://fairness_ontology.com/papers/> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix vcard: <http://www.w3.org/2001/vcard-rdf/3.0#> .
@prefix bibtex: <http://www.edutella.org/bibtex#> .
@base <http://fairness_ontology.com/indiv/> .

<http://fairness_ontology.com/indiv> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
                                       owl:imports core: , papers:.





###  http://fairness_ontology.com/ValueUnfairness
:ValueUnfairness rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                          :AmortizedGranularityMetric ,
                          :UserFocusedMetric ;
                 :isProposedBy papers:2017_YAO ;
                 :isUsedBy papers:2018_FARNADI ;
                 :measures :EqualOpportunity .


###  http://fairness_ontology.com/Variance
:Variance rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                   :AmortizedGranularityMetric ,
                   :ImpactFocusedMetric ,
                   :ItemFocusedMetric ,
                   :SingleGranularityMetric ,
                   :TreatmentFocusedMetric ,
                   :UserFocusedMetric ;
          :isProposedBy papers:2017_LIN ;
          :isUsedBy papers:2018_KAMISHIMA ,
                    papers:2019_RASTEGARPANAH ,
                    papers:2021_WU ;
          :measures :FairRecommendationForCustomer ,
                    :QualityWeightedFairness ,
                    :UniformFairness ;
          skos:definition "Variance is \"population size independent\" (applicable to any number of users) and satisfies \"continuity\" (any slight change in allocation shows up in the index), but affected by the unit of measure. Example: if an algorithm gives 1,3,5 dollars respectively to three users, variance is 4. If it gives 100,300,500 pennies each, variance is 40,000 (10000 worse than before) [1984_JAIN]. Coefficent of variation (i.e., variance/mean) would not be affected by the change of unit of measure. Furthermore, variance (and Coefficent of variation) does not satisfy boundedness (the index should be bounded between 0 and 1 for comparrison and interpretability)" .


