###  http://fairness_ontology.com/PredictiveEquality
:PredictiveEquality rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                             :ObservationalFairnessNotion ,
                             :SeparationFairnessNotion ;
                    :hasBeenAppliedTo :LawAndJustice ;
                    :isEquivalentTo :FalsePositiveErrorRateBalance ;
                    :isProposedBy papers:2016_HARDT ;
                    :refersTo :Classification ;
                    skos:definition "a predictor that satisfy both predictive equality and equal opportunity, also satisfy equalized odds." .


###  http://fairness_ontology.com/PredictiveParity
:PredictiveParity rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                           :ObservationalFairnessNotion ,
                           :SufficiencyFairnessNotion ;
                  :extends :Calibration ;
                  :hasBeenAppliedTo :LawAndJustice ;
                  :isProposedBy papers:2016_DIETERICH ,
                                papers:2017_CHOULDECHOVA ,
                                papers:2017_KUSNER ;
                  :isUsedBy papers:2020_MANSOURY ;
                  :refersTo :Classification ;
                  skos:definition "2020_MANSOURY use precision (predictive parity) as a benchmarck metric to evaluate the fraction of the recommended items shown to the users that are part of the users’ profile in the test set. While predictive parity and calibration look like very similar criteria, test fair scores can fail to satisfy predictive parity at a given threshold. This is because the relationship between predictive parity and test fairness depends on the conditional distribution of R | A , which can differ across groups in ways that result in PPV imbalance. In the simple case where R itself is binary (Y^), a score that is test fair will also satisfy predictive parity." .

###  http://fairness_ontology.com/PredictedReplacements
:PredictedReplacements rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                :OutputRewriting ;
                       :isProposedBy papers:2022_TOKPO ;
                       :scientificArtifactDescription "Use LIME (2016_RIBEIRO) to identify tokens responsible for bias in an output and predict new tokens for replacement based on the latent representations of the original sentence" .

