###  http://fairness_ontology.com/FairRecommendationForCustomer
:FairRecommendationForCustomer rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                        :IndividualFairnessNotion ,
                                        :ObservationalFairnessNotion ;
                               :isMeasuredBy :Variance ;
                               :isProposedBy papers:2021_WU ;
                               :scientificArtifactDescription "The  recommendation is fair for customers if each customer receives recommendation results with the same Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain (NDCG) value,i.e., $NDCG(u_1) = NDCG(u_2), \\forall u_1, u_2 \\in U$. NDCG is a ranking metric used to evaluate recommendation systems by measuring the quality of the ranked list of recommended items. It assesses a system's ability to place the most relevant items at the top of the list, with higher scores indicating a better ranking. NDCG is calculated by dividing the Discounted Cumulative Gain (DCG) of the actual recommendation list by the DCG of an ideal list, resulting in a score between 0 and 1." .


###  http://fairness_ontology.com/FairScore
:FairScore rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                    :ScoreBasedFairnessMetric ;
           :isProposedBy papers:2022_QIAN ;
           :isUsedBy papers:2025_RUSSO ;
           :measures :CounterfactualFairness ;
           :scientificArtifactDescription """Is a counterfactual fairness metric that measures “the change in model predictions between the original evaluation dataset and the perturbation augmented version”, with a comparatively lower score indicating a fairer model. The fairscore of a classifier is defined as the percentage of predictions that differ when the input is demographically altered. More formally, for a perturber model $f_P$ and a text snippet $x$, let $\\tilde{x} \\sim f_P(x)$ be the demographically altered perturber output. A classifier $f_C$ exhibits bias if, for some input $x$ and its demographically perturbed version $\\tilde{x}$, the predictions differ, i.e.,
\\[
f_C(x) \\neq f_C(\\tilde{x}).
\\]
Given a classifier $f_C$ and an evaluation set $X$, we define the \\emph{fairscore} $F_S$ as
\\[
F_S(f_C, X)
=
\\frac{\\left| \\{ x \\in X \\mid f_C(x) \\neq f_C(\\tilde{x}) \\} \\right|}{|X|}.
\\]""" .


###  http://fairness_ontology.com/FairnessThroughAwareness
:FairnessThroughAwareness rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                   :IndividualFairnessNotion ,
                                   :ObservationalFairnessNotion ;
                          owl:sameAs :IndividualFairness ;
                          :hasBeenAppliedTo :Education ,
                                            :Healthcare ,
                                            :LawAndJustice ;
                          :isEquivalentTo :IndividualFairness ;
                          :isMeasuredBy :ConsistencyScore ,
                                        :IndividualConsistencyScore ,
                                        :LowIndividualConsistencyCount ,
                                        :ProportionalConsistencyScore ,
                                        :RelativeLInfiniteMetric ,
                                        :SimilarityRobustnessOfIndividualConsistency ,
                                        :TotalVariationNorm ;
                          :isProposedBy papers:2012_DWORK ;
                          :isUsedBy papers:2018_YONA ,
                                    papers:2019_LAHOTI ,
                                    papers:2020_YUROCHKIN ,
                                    papers:2025_GAO ;
                          :refersTo :Classification ;
                          :scientificArtifactDescription """An algorithm is fair if it gives similar predictions to similar individuals
Awareness is based on the Lipschitz condition, which requires that any two individuals 
$x$ and $y$ at distance $d(x,y) \\in [0,1]$ map to distributions $M(x)$ and $M(y)$, respectively, such that the statistical distance between them is at most $d(x,y)$:
\\[
D(M(x_1), M(x_2)) \\le d(x_1, x_2), 
\\quad \\forall x_1, x_2 \\in E .
\\]

Here, $M(x)$ denotes the probability distribution of the output for individual $x$, 
$E$ is a dataset, $D(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ returns the statistical distance between 
$M(x_1)$ and $M(x_2)$, and $d(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ denotes a distance between individuals 
$x_1$ and $x_2$, defined using a pre-specified, domain-dependent notion of similarity.
\\cite{2025_WALLER}""" .


