                                      :ObservationalFairnessNotion ,
                                      :SeparationFairnessNotion ;
                             :extends :EqualOpportunity ,
                                      :StatisticalParity ;
                             :hasBeenAppliedTo :LawAndJustice ;
                             :isProposedBy papers:2019_BEUTEL ;
                             :refersTo :Classification ;
                             skos:definition "Extends both Statistical parity and Equal opportunity (although considers FPRs instead of TPRs)" .


###  http://fairness_ontology.com/ConditionalProcedureAccuracyEquality
:ConditionalProcedureAccuracyEquality rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                               :ObservationalFairnessNotion ,
                                               :SeparationFairnessNotion ;
                                      :extends :EqualOpportunity ,
                                               :PredictiveEquality ;
                                      :hasBeenAppliedTo :Healthcare ,
                                                        :LawAndJustice ;
                                      :isEquivalentTo :EqualizedOdds ;
                                      :isMeasuredBy :OutcomeUnfairness ;
                                      :isProposedBy papers:2018_VANMASSENHOVE ,
                                                    papers:2021_BERK ;
                                      :refersTo :Classification ;
                                      skos:definition "Equal opportunity  and Predictive equality  i.e., if you satisfy Equalized odds, than you also satisfy Equal opportunity" .


###  http://fairness_ontology.com/ConditionalStatisticalParity
:ConditionalStatisticalParity rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                       :IndependenceFairnessNotion ,
                                       :ObservationalFairnessNotion ;
                              :extends :FairnessThroughUnawareness ,
                                       :StatisticalParity ;
                              :hasBeenAppliedTo :Healthcare ,
                                                :LawAndJustice ;
                              :isProposedBy papers:2017_CORBETT-DAVIES ;
                              :refersTo :Classification ;
                              skos:definition "Conditional statistical parity is closely related to the idea of fairness through blindness (i.e., unawareness[Ndr]), in which one attempts to create fair algorithms by prohibiting use of protected attributes, such as race. However, as frequently noted, it is difficult to restrict to “legitimate” features that do not at least partially correlate with race and other protected attributes, and so one cannot be completely “blind” to the sensitive information. Moreover, unlike the other definitions of fairness, this one does not necessarily re- duce racial disparities. Conditional statistical parity mitigates these limitations of the blindness approach while preserving its intuitive appeal. [2017_CORBETT-DAVIES]" .


###  http://fairness_ontology.com/ConditionalUseAccuracyEquality
:ConditionalUseAccuracyEquality rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                         :ObservationalFairnessNotion ,
                                         :SufficiencyFairnessNotion ;
                                :extends :PredictiveParity ,
                                         :TestFairness ;
                                :hasBeenAppliedTo :LawAndJustice ;
                                :isProposedBy papers:2021_BERK ;
                                :refersTo :Classification ;
                                skos:definition "similar to Conditional procedure accuracy equality (=Equalized odds) but conditioning on predicted values (i.e, P' and N') instead of the actual ones (P and N)  Also extends Test fairness/Predictive parity that just looks to the TP" .



###  http://fairness_ontology.com/CDA
:CDA rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
              :CounterfactualAugmentation ;
     :isProposedBy papers:2020_LU ;
     :scientificArtifactDescription "Use CDA to mitigate occupation-gender bias, creating matched pairs by flipping gendered (e.g., “he” and “she”) or definitionally gendered (e.g., “king” and “queen”) words, while preserving grammatical and semantic correctness, under the definition that an unbiased model should consider each sentence in a pair equally." .


###  http://fairness_ontology.com/CDS
